{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1546225-curseborne-npc-ideas","id":1546225,"title":"Curseborne NPC ideas","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Curseborne"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-11-20T19:52:24-08:00","id":1546225,"title":"Curseborne NPC ideas","content":"I've been trying to come up with some npc ideas for a chronicle I'm working on. I'm trying to brainstorm ideas for what kinds of npcs would work for each family. Some families are fairly easy to figure out, but others I'm really struggling with. I don't want to list out every curseborne in the city, but I figure I should have an idea of who the major npcs could be and what might be useful to the characters. I have some ideas for the Dead and the Hungry, but I'm still working on the rest. Let me know if I'm completely off the park for any of the families, as I mentioned, some of them are particularly hard to figure out how they fit into the world. I'd also love to see npc ideas I might have missed.<br /> <br /> The Dead<br /> - Furies (anger) - martial arts dojo, gang member, activist, musician (fade = makes decisions on conflicts and punish rule breakers)<br /> - Mavens (excitement) - club owner, thrill seeking activities, amusement park, bdsm club<br /> - Poltergeists (fear) - haunted house, mortuary, vigilante<br /> - Shades (grief) - grief counselor, prison guard/warden/counselor, prosecutor<br /> - Wardens (desperation?) - cop, doctor, social worker<br /> - Zed - assassin, angel of mercy<br /> <br /> The Hungry<br /> - Ascetics (memories) - scientist, professor, librarian, antiquarian<br /> - Black Hearts (emotions) - cult leader, therapist<br /> - Gaki (ghosts) - mortician, exorcist, graveyard keeper, hospital janitor<br /> - Heirs (blood) - old money, crime boss, ceo, politician<br /> - House Bathory (blood) - celebrity, art gallery, bdsm club<br /> - Iscariots (flesh) - activist, social worker, public defender<br /> - Vorare (souls) - cult leader, cop, social worker","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13542-shadowdragon","name":"Shadowdragon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-21T07:51:09-08:00","id":1546243,"title":"","content":"It might be helpful to have a clearer idea of what you're looking for here, and what kind of campaign you're building.<br /> <br /> For what it's worth I definitely *wouldn't* try to detail every single Family in the game in the early planning stages, I'd pick the ones that are most interesting to the players (including you). You need to know what the PCs' Families are up to, and what the Families you're interested in are doing, but if neither you nor your players give a crap about the Chimera, you don't need to think too much about them.<br /> <br /> If you're interested in who the major NPCs are and what's useful for the PCs, then I'd be inclined to work backwards from your current method. Instead of saying &quot;okay, what will the Furies be doing in this city&quot; think to yourself &quot;okay, what sorts of NPCs do I see the players interacting with&quot; and then ask yourself which Families those characters should be in.<br /> <br /> For example, on your list above &quot;BDSM Club&quot; pops up a couple of times; do you actually want a BDSM club to be a prominent feature of your game? If so, cool. Detail it, give it a role in the local Accursed community, and detail its owners. But if you don't especially want that, then there's not much point having a Bathorite who owns a BDSM club just for the sake of having a Bathorite NPC.<br /> <br /> I'd start by listing the roles you want NPCs to play in the game - information broker, sleazy exec, corrupt politician, local gang leader, firebrand activist, whatever and then assign Families from there. And I'd have kind of an eighty-twenty rule on archetypal vs contrary-to-type characters. That is, if you're going with the five examples I threw together above, you might make the Information Broker a Vodnik, the sleazy exec an Heir, and the local gang leader a Lykan, but then do something unexpected with the firebrand activist, making them a Faceless or a Gaki or something.<br /> <br /> But ultimately you can't design NPCs for a game unless you know what game you're designing those NPCs for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28625-nobody-special","name":"Nobody Special","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"219"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}